tal\", \"id\": \"D010634\", \"label\": \"Chemical\"}, {\"text\": \"idiopathic epilepsy\", \"id\": \"C562694\", \"label\": \"Disease\"}]","filtered_entities":[{"text":"idiopathic epilepsy","id":"C562694","label":"Disease"},{"text":"idiopathic epilepsy","id":"C562694","label":"Disease"},{"text":"idiopathic epilepsy","id":"C562694","label":"Disease"},{"text":"Seizure","id":"D012640","label":"Disease"},{"text":"seizure","id":"D012640","label":"Disease"},{"text":"seizures","id":"D012640","label":"Disease"},{"text":"seizure","id":"D012640","label":"Disease"},{"text":"seizures","id":"D012640","label":"Disease"},{"text":"seizure","id":"D012640","label":"Disease"},{"text":"lethargy","id":"D053609","label":"Disease"},{"text":"inappetence","id":"-1","label":"Disease"},{"text":"idiopathic epilepsy","id":"C562694","label":"Disease"},{"text":"Levetiracetam","id":"C026098","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"phenobarbital","id":"D010634","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"levetiracetam","id":"C026098","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"phenobarbital","id":"D010634","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"phenobarbital","id":"D010634","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"phenobarbital","id":"D010634","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"levetiracetam","id":"C026098","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"levetiracetam","id":"C026098","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"levetiracetam","id":"C026098","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"levetiracetam","id":"C026098","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"levetiracetam","id":"C026098","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"levetiracetam","id":"C026098","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"levetiracetam","id":"C026098","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"levetiracetam","id":"C026098","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"phenobarbital","id":"D010634","label":"Chemical"}],"head":"phenobarbital","head_id":"D010634","pairs":[{"head":"Levetiracetam","tail":"idiopathic epilepsy","head_id":"C026098","tail_id":"C562694"},{"head":"Levetiracetam","tail":"Seizure","head_id":"C026098","tail_id":"D012640"},{"head":"Levetiracetam","tail":"lethargy","head_id":"C026098","tail_id":"D053609"},{"head":"Levetiracetam","tail":"inappetence","head_id":"C026098","tail_id":"-1"},{"head":"phenobarbital","tail":"idiopathic epilepsy","head_id":"D010634","tail_id":"C562694"},{"head":"phenobarbital","tail":"Seizure","head_id":"D010634","tail_id":"D012640"},{"head":"phenobarbital","tail":"lethargy","head_id":"D010634","tail_id":"D053609"},{"head":"phenobarbital","tail":"inappetence","head_id":"D010634","tail_id":"-1"}],"relations":["C026098 | D053609","C026098 | -1"],"result":"~","tail":"inappetence","tail_id":"-1","text":"Levetiracetam as an adjunct to phenobarbital treatment in cats with suspected idiopathic epilepsy. OBJECTIVE: To assess pharmacokinetics, efficacy, and tolerability of oral levetiracetam administered as an adjunct to phenobarbital treatment in cats with poorly controlled suspected idiopathic epilepsy. DESIGN-Open-label, noncomparative clinical trial. ANIMALS: 12 cats suspected to have idiopathic epilepsy that was poorly controlled with phenobarbital or that had unacceptable adverse effects when treated with phenobarbital. PROCEDURES: Cats were treated with levetiracetam (20 mg/kg [9.1 mg/lb], PO, q 8 h). After a minimum of 1 week of treatment, serum levetiracetam concentrations were measured before and 2, 4, and 6 hours after drug administration, and maximum and minimum serum concentrations and elimination half-life were calculated. Seizure frequencies before and after initiation of levetiracetam treatment were compared, and adverse effects were recorded. RESULTS: Median maximum serum levetiracetam concentration was 25.5 microg/mL, median minimum serum levetiracetam concentration was 8.3 microg/mL, and median elimination half-life was 2.9 hours. Median seizure frequency prior to treatment with levetiracetam (2.1 seizures/mo) was significantly higher than median seizure frequency after initiation of levetiracetam treatment (0.42 seizures/mo), and 7 of 10 cats were classified as having responded to levetiracetam treatment (ie, reduction in seizure frequency of >or=50%). Two cats had transient lethargy and inappetence. CONCLUSIONS AND CLINICAL RELEVANCE: Results suggested that levetiracetam is well tolerated in cats and may be useful as an adjunct to phenobarbital treatment in cats with idiopathic epilepsy.","metrics":{"precision":0.5,"recall":1.0,"f1":0.6666666666666666,"rel_tp":1,"rel_fp":1,"rel_fn":0}}